.\" -*- coding: UTF-8 -*-
.\" mpmtr-app-http(1). Keep in sync with cmd/mpmtr-app-http/main.go.
.TH MPMTR\-APP\-HTTP 1 "November 2026" "mpmtr" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mpmtr\-app\-http \- application-check plugin for mpmtr: does the server answer HTTP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mpmtr \-\-app http
.RI [ options ]
.IR target [: port ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mpmtr\-app\-http
is a plugin of
.BR mpmtr (1).
It is not run by hand:
.B mpmtr \-\-app http
starts it and hands it connections.
It has no options of its own.
.PP
.B mpmtr
measures with bare TCP SYN packets, which tell only that a server accepts
connections.
With this plugin every measured source port also gets, every
.B \-\-app\-interval
(default 10 s), one real HTTP request to the server that port leads to.
A server that accepts connections and does not answer HTTP is then seen as
such: its checks fail while TCP answers, and after three failed checks in a
row it is DOWN.
.SH WHAT ONE CHECK DOES
.IP 1. 4
.B mpmtr
opens a kernel TCP connection from the measured source port to the target:
the same addresses and ports its probes use, so a load balancer leads the
connection to the same server.
.IP 2. 4
The plugin gets the connected socket and sends
.RS 4
.PP
.EX
HEAD / HTTP/1.0
Host: \fIaddress of the target\fP
User\-Agent: mpmtr
.EE
.RE
.IP 3. 4
It reads one line of the answer.
A line that starts with
.B HTTP/
is a passed check, whatever the status code.
.IP 4. 4
.B mpmtr
closes the connection with RST.
.PP
The whole check must fit in
.B \-\-app\-timeout
(default 5 s).
.SH RESULTS
.TP
passed
The server sent an HTTP status line.
Counts as an answer of that port and server; its RTT is the time of the TCP
handshake of this connection.
.TP
.RB "failed, stage " send
The request could not be written (the server reset the connection).
.TP
.RB "failed, stage " answer
No line came before the timeout, the server closed the connection without
one, or the line is not an HTTP status line.
.PP
The checks are counted apart from the TCP probes: a failed one is shown as
.BI app\-failed: N / M
in the flags and as
.BR app_failed " of " app_checks
in JSON, with a rate of its own, and is not part of the TCP failure share.
Three failed checks in a row make the port DOWN, reason
.BR app_down .
A connection that was refused or never established is counted by
.B mpmtr
itself, before the plugin sees anything.
.PP
To see every check with its stage and error text, set
.B MPMTR_DEBUG_APP=1
and read standard error.
.SH WHAT IT DOES NOT CHECK
.IP \(bu 2
The status code.
404, 500 and a redirect all pass: the check answers "is an HTTP server
alive here", not "does the site work".
.IP \(bu 2
Virtual hosts.
The
.B Host
header carries the target's address, not the name you gave on the command
line, so a server sees a request for an unknown site.
.IP \(bu 2
TLS.
The request is plain text.
On a TLS port (443) the outcome depends on the server: some answer a
plain-text request with an HTTP error line (the check passes), some close
the connection (the check fails).
Use it on plain HTTP ports.
.PP
A check of your own protocol is a small program: see
.B docs/WRITING_PLUGINS.md
in the source tree.
.SH EXAMPLES
Every route to a web server, each measured port checked over HTTP:
.RS
.EX
mpmtr \-\-app http example.com:80
.EE
.RE
.PP
Every server behind an anycast address, checked every 30 s:
.RS
.EX
mpmtr \-\-anycast \-\-app http \-\-app\-interval 30s example.com:80
.EE
.RE
.SH FILES
.B mpmtr
looks for the executable
.B mpmtr\-app\-http
next to itself, then in
.BR PATH .
.SH SECURITY
The plugin runs with the privileges
.B mpmtr
has.
It sends nothing but the request above, and only over connections
.B mpmtr
gave it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mpmtr (1),
.BR mpmtr\-hop\-snmp (1)
.PP
.B docs/PLUGINS.md
(the plugin protocol),
.B docs/WRITING_PLUGINS.md
(how to write a plugin).
